  [PASS] 48c 🔴 AGAINST THE HYPOTHESIS THIS SCRIPT WAS WRITTEN TO TEST -- the published headline of items 48/69 is NOT a selection effect.  Re-isolating relatively, with no third 2MRS galaxy inside 2 to 8 times the pair's own separation, does not move the amplitude: it sits at 1.8-2.0 with no trend while the sample shrinks four-fold and the median separation falls from 270 to 104 kpc.  A = 1.74 is CONFIRMED and the liability stands.  (For the ALFALFA dwarf pairs of item 47 the same criterion DID move it, from 1.79 to 1.12 +/- 0.29 -- a 2.6 sigma difference from this sample, recorded as unresolved)   (A(isolated deep-MOND) across F = 2, 3, 5, 8: 1.76, 1.76, 1.89, 1.87; at F = 5 (N = 1830, median r_p = 141 kpc) A = 1.89 +/- 0.05, 19 sigma above 1 and +8% from the published 1.74.  The framework would need 13x the K-band baryonic mass)
  [PASS] 48d AND LambdaCDM LANDS ON ITS OWN PREDICTION over the same scan, which is what makes 48c a statement about the framework rather than about the sample.  Abundance-matched NFW halos give A = 0.90-1.06 with no free parameter once the pairs are properly isolated, while the framework's best case needs a factor of two in velocity   (LambdaCDM A across F = 2, 3, 5, 8: 0.91, 0.90, 0.95, 0.94; at F = 5, 0.95 +/- 0.02 (2.3 sigma from 1) against the framework's 1.89 +/- 0.05)
  
  what would close the framework's gap at F = 5?  A = 1.89 means 13x the assumed baryonic mass (v ~ M_b^1/4), i.e. Upsilon_K = 7.6 instead of 0.6, which no stellar population reaches; or gas 12 times the stellar mass, which L* early types do not have in cold form; or a hot intra-pair medium, which is the one baryonic reservoir 2MRS cannot see and the one the framework is entitled to ask for -- but a 1e12 Msun pair does not carry 1e13 Msun of hot gas either.
  the no-isolation row is printed for scale only -- it is a sample of group members, not of pairs, and its A = 3.63 is what an unfiltered catalogue gives.
  [PASS] 48e 🔴 WHAT DOES NOT MOVE: the framework's own external-field branch.  Whatever the isolation, the quasi-Newtonian G_eff = nu(e_N)G prediction is far below the measured dispersion, so the framework needs these pairs to be on the ISOLATED deep-MOND branch -- which for L* pairs at hundreds of kpc means an external field far smaller than the large-scale-structure value.  This is the same finding as item 47's 47g, on a mass scale two decades higher   (framework with e_N = 0.03: A = 7.85 at no isolation and 2.62 +/- 0.06 at d3 > 5 r_p, 26.0 sigma above 1)

  both footings on the d3 > 5 r_p sample (N = 1830, median r_p = 141 kpc):
    canonical : A(isolated deep-MOND) = 1.89 +/- 0.05 (+0.276 dex), interloper fraction 0.17
    alt       : A(isolated deep-MOND) = 1.80 +/- 0.04 (+0.255 dex), interloper fraction 0.17
  gas is still not counted in M_b (2MRS is K-band only).  Adding 20% of M* in gas raises the framework's prediction by 0.02 dex in velocity and moves A down by 5%; it cannot be the whole story either way.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MUTATION CONTROLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M1 mutation control: scrambling which galaxy each pair member is paired with must drive the fitted PAIR fraction to the floor on the strict sample too, or the strict sample has no pairs left in it   (pair fraction 1-f on the strict sample: real 0.82 (sigma 201 km/s) -> scrambled 0.01 (sigma 5 km/s))
  [PASS] M2 mutation control: a_0 x 100 must move the required amplitude by exactly 0.5 dex on the strict sample, or the amplitudes above are insensitive to the physics they claim to test   (log10(A_true/A_x100) = +0.500, predicted +0.500)
  [PASS] M3 injection-recovery control: mock velocity differences built from the same per-pair predictions with a known amplitude must come back with it.  A biased estimator would make the whole scan meaningless   (injected A = 1.50, f_int = 0.20; recovered A = 1.52 +/- 0.03, f_int = 0.21)
  
  the relative criterion is blind to the pair's own dv by construction -- it looks only at THIRD galaxies -- but the check is worth making explicitly:
    F = 0: median |dv| = 398 km/s, median r_p = 591 kpc, median log M_b = 11.06
    F = 5: median |dv| = 155 km/s, median r_p = 141 kpc, median log M_b = 11.20
  [PASS] M4 control: the strict sample must differ from the loose one in SEPARATION and not in baryonic mass, or the amplitude change is a mass effect rather than an isolation effect   (median log M_b(pair): all 11.06 -> strict 11.20; median r_p 591 -> 141 kpc)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE:
   * it does NOT retire items 48/69's liability, which is what it set out to test.  The amplitude is stable
     under a four-fold tightening of the isolation on the axis that matters, so the offset is not the loose
     absolute veto's doing.  h48_h69's number stands and this script strengthens it.
   * the SEPARATION between the two laws is the result: on well-isolated major pairs LambdaCDM's
     abundance-matched halos need no adjustment and the framework's best case needs a factor of two in
     velocity.  That is the sharpest negative these pair samples produce.
   * it does NOT touch item 48b's other finding, that the KT2017 catalogue cannot be used at all because its
     group-finder selects on the velocity difference being measured.  That stands.
   * the framework's external-field branch remains excluded at both mass scales (item 47's 47g and 48e here).
     That is a statement AGAINST the framework's own honest prediction, not for it: it forces the isolated
     deep-MOND branch on pairs whose internal field is well below the large-scale-structure external field.
   * THE UNRESOLVED CROSS-ITEM TENSION: item 47's ALFALFA dwarf pairs give A = 1.12 +/- 0.29 under the same
     relative criterion where these major pairs give 1.89 +/- 0.05.  Either the dwarf sample's 53 pairs are
     noise, or the offset grows with mass -- which would point at the baryons 2MRS cannot see (hot gas around
     L* early types) rather than at gravity.  Deciding it needs an HI-complete major-pair sample.
   * remaining systematics unchanged from h48_h69: 2MRS's depth (fainter companions are not vetoed), cz/H0
     distances, Upsilon_K = 0.6 assumed, no gas in M_b, circular orbits assumed.  Every one of these except
     the gas pushes the framework's requirement UP, not down.

